<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The hard golden age of the 1920s: enormous Tour stages, primitive mountain roads, reversible rear wheels and riders acting as their own mechanics."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1920-1930/mountain-stop.webp"/><title>Cycle racing’s hard golden age · 1920–1929 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1920-1930/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1920-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1920–1929</p><h1>Cycle racing’s hard <em>golden age</em></h1></div><p>Modern road racing took shape while riders were still treated as a mixture of athletes, mechanics and travelling endurance specialists.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1920-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/mountain-stop.webp`} alt="Riders with spare tyres over their shoulders at a mountain pass"/><figcaption>A rest on a rough mountain road. The spare tyres over their shoulders say as much about the period as the riders’ wool jerseys. The image’s precise date is not documented in the archive.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead"><p class="section-label">After the war</p><p>Europe was scarred, the roads were worn, and many riders had never returned home. Even so, cycle racing grew during the 1920s into one of the continent’s largest popular spectacles.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1920-facts" aria-label="Facts about the Tour de France in this period">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">A generation marked by war</p><h2>The sport returns to a changed Europe</h2></div><div><p>The Tour de France was not held from 1915 to 1918. When it returned in 1919, the peloton passed ruined villages, military cemeteries and landscapes in which the war was still visible.</p><p>The bicycle industry had changed too. Factories had produced military equipment, and materials were difficult to obtain. Several marques joined the La Sportive consortium, whose teams shaped the first post-war years until manufacturers could once again build their own professional squads.</p><p>Tour director Henri Desgrange cultivated the ideal of the self-reliant rider. The race was meant to find not only the fastest man, but the character who could endure hunger, cold, solitude and mechanical problems with the least possible help.</p></div></section>
<figure class="decade1920-wide"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/riders-at-finish.webp`} alt="Riders and spectators gathered at a bicycle race" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The riders became moving advertisements for bicycle factories and modern heroes to a growing public. Archive photograph of uncertain date.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Beyond a modern yardstick</p><h2>A whole working week measured in kilometres</h2></div><div><p>In the 1920s, a stage of more than 300 kilometres was not unusual. The 1920 Tour covered about 5,500 kilometres in only 15 stages. On race days, riders could be out from before sunrise until well into the evening.</p><p>The longest Tour was held in 1926: 5,745 kilometres over 17 stages. Belgian Lucien Buysse won after almost 239 hours of riding. On the decisive stage from Bayonne to Luchon, he attacked in rain, sleet and wet snow over climbs including the Aubisque and Tourmalet.</p><p>Buysse embodied the period’s ideal: the strongest was not necessarily the most explosive, but the man who could continue when the others were close to breaking. In 1924, the stage from Les Sables-d’Olonne to Bayonne measured 482 kilometres — longer than two present-day Tour stages put together.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1920-mountains"><figure><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/walking-mountain.webp`} alt="Rider pushing his bicycle on a snow-covered mountain pass" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>When gearing and road no longer matched, the bicycle race continued on foot.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/rider-in-snow.webp`} alt="Rider with spare tyres on a rough road in snow-covered mountains" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Spare tyres, a bag and supplies accompanied the rider through the mountains. The archive date is uncertain.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">The steel bicycle</p><h2>A refined working tool</h2></div><div><p>Professional bicycles of the 1920s were not simply clumsy iron machines. The best had thin steel tubes, narrow tyres and competition-developed parts, though they were heavier and far less efficient than today’s racing bicycles.</p><p>Rims were often wooden and tubular tyres were glued in place. This could produce a light and comfortable wheel, but rain and braking heat affected both wood and glue. The simple rim brakes required the rider to read the road early; a sudden stop on a wet descent was seldom possible.</p><p>Freewheels were no longer unusual. The crucial problem was the lack of a practical gear change.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1920-gear"><figure><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/early-derailleur.webp`} alt="Early mechanical gear system at the rear wheel" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Early gear systems existed, but Henri Desgrange kept practical derailleurs out of the Tour de France for a long time.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">When the rear wheel was the gear lever</p><h2>Stop. Loosen. Turn. Tighten.</h2><p>Many racing bicycles had a reversible rear wheel with one sprocket on each side: a smaller one for flat roads and a larger one for the mountains. Before the climb, the rider had to loosen the wing nuts, turn the wheel around, replace the chain and align the wheel again.</p><p>With cold or exhausted fingers, even a wing nut could become an opponent. If the wheel sat crooked, the tyre could rub against the frame or the chain could come off. Changing gear was not merely technique — it was a small mechanic’s test in the middle of the race.</p></div></section>
<figure class="decade1920-wide repair"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1920-1930/christophe-roadside.webp`} alt="Eugène Christophe repairing his bicycle at the roadside" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Eugène Christophe carrying out a roadside repair. The pump, spare tyres and tools were part of the rider’s luggage.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Spare parts on the body</p><h2>No radio. No guarantee of help.</h2></div><div><p>Team managers, mechanics and cars already existed, so the 1920s were not entirely an age before the team car. But help was far less accessible, and the Tour’s rules continued to be based on personal self-reliance.</p><p>Spare tubular tyres were carried in a figure of eight over the shoulders. After a puncture, the rider had to tear off the tyre, fit a new one and inflate it with a hand pump. He might also need to join a chain, straighten a wheel or make a damaged pedal usable.</p><p>If he stood alone between two villages, the choice was simple: repair it with the tools he carried — or start walking. That may be where 1920s cycle racing shows its true face: technically refined and widely celebrated, yet still unforgivingly close to the pioneering years.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature"><div><p class="section-label">Stories from the period</p><h2>The individual moments</h2><p>The period provides the overview. From here, you can continue to the individual stories about people and events in the 1920s.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links">{relatedStories.map(s=><a href={`${base}/en/stories/${s.slug}/`}><span>Related story · {s.year}</span><strong>{s.title}</strong><b>Read the story →</b></a>)}</div></section>
